WEBVTT

1
00:00:00.350 --> 00:00:03.570
Prove your standard: buyer packs, training and audits.

2
00:00:03.920 --> 00:00:08.930
This is the video for module three of Product Standardisation and Compliance.

3
00:00:10.180 --> 00:00:18.780
When a supermarket or hotel considers a new supplier, the buyer must convince her manager. Clear documents make her job easy.

4
00:00:20.030 --> 00:00:27.460
If quality drops when you are away, the standard lives in you, not in the business. So you train others until it does not.

5
00:00:28.710 --> 00:00:33.190
Then you audit yourself, checking that what your papers say is what really happens.

6
00:00:34.440 --> 00:00:38.230
And you choose the one gap that matters most for the next 90 days.

7
00:00:39.480 --> 00:00:58.340
Keep this pack as PDFs on your phone. A data sheet summarises your spec for buyers. For example, Funmi weighs a filled jar at 160 grams. 24 jars plus a 300 gram carton gives 4,140 grams, about 4.1 kilograms.

8
00:00:58.690 --> 00:01:01.720
Send copies with a watermark, never originals.

9
00:01:02.970 --> 00:01:11.010
Answer supplier forms truthfully. A dated plan, like registration expected by March, is better than a false yes.

10
00:01:11.360 --> 00:01:16.510
Send samples from a normal batch, and keep retained samples from every batch you sell.

11
00:01:17.760 --> 00:01:23.960
A skills matrix lists people down the side and tasks across the top. 0 means not trained.

12
00:01:25.210 --> 00:01:28.480
1 means trained, but works only under supervision.

13
00:01:29.730 --> 00:01:32.440
2 means signed off, working alone to standard.

14
00:01:33.690 --> 00:01:43.120
3 means she can train others. Aim for at least two people at level 2 on every critical task, so one person's illness never stops production.

15
00:01:44.370 --> 00:01:54.610
For example, Chioma's sign-off rule for handles is three batches of 10 bags in a row. Handles must be 60 cm, plus or minus 1 cm.

16
00:01:55.860 --> 00:01:58.890
Each batch may have no more than 1 bag needing rework.

17
00:02:00.140 --> 00:02:05.530
Amina's first batch has 3 reworks. That is 30%, so the count restarts.

18
00:02:06.780 --> 00:02:16.640
Her next three batches have 1, 0 and 1. Chioma signs and dates the matrix, moving her to level 2, with a refresher check in three months.

19
00:02:17.890 --> 00:02:23.780
Decide the rule before training starts, so it is fair, and Amina can see the evidence herself.

20
00:02:25.030 --> 00:02:34.480
Train and sign off the safety steps before production steps. A learner should know where the lid, first aid box and exit are before she touches the pot.

21
00:02:35.730 --> 00:02:48.440
Say this often. If anything does not match the spec, stop, set it aside and tell me. You will never be blamed for stopping. If people fear blame, they hide problems, and hidden problems reach customers.

22
00:02:49.690 --> 00:02:54.880
And teach why each record matters. An entry made from memory is not evidence.

23
00:02:56.130 --> 00:03:04.340
Every quarter, gather your spec, SOPs, label and records. Watch one batch being made without helping, and note where practice differs.

24
00:03:05.590 --> 00:03:09.240
Pick three finished units at random and check them against the spec.

25
00:03:10.490 --> 00:03:14.920
Trace one batch backwards, from a sold unit's batch code to its materials.

26
00:03:16.170 --> 00:03:26.160
For example, Funmi scores 9 out of 16, about 56%. Traceability scores 0, because supplier batch numbers were never written down.

27
00:03:27.410 --> 00:03:42.430
Ask of each gap: does a buyer I want require it, and could it harm a customer? A pharmacy chain she wants asks about recalls, so traceability is her priority. Free fixes, like an old SOP on the wall, happen this week.

28
00:03:43.680 --> 00:03:47.990
The plan has three blocks: set up, run and record, then check and prove.

29
00:03:49.240 --> 00:03:57.410
In week one she adds supplier name and supplier batch number columns to her records, in a new record book for about 2,000 naira.

30
00:03:58.660 --> 00:04:05.360
In week two she retrains both helpers and adds a retained sample shelf, for about 5,000 naira.

31
00:04:05.710 --> 00:04:14.450
Near the end she runs a mock recall, tracing a batch to its materials and customers within 2 hours. That is strong proof for any buyer.

32
00:04:15.700 --> 00:04:27.270
Now build your buyer pack and start a retained sample box. Make your skills matrix and display your stop rule. Then run your audit and write your 90-day plan with one measurable target.

33
00:04:27.620 --> 00:04:29.560
Continue with the lessons below.
